- op: discuss  # 640f
  at: '2026-07-16T20:45:33Z'  # 640f
  by: agent  # 640f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 640f
  clock: 3  # 640f
  nonce: 640f  # 640f
  message: "Fixed in PR #694 (dev b6bfe4951a). Added a resolution strategy in rust.py _extract_edges_from_file: when a method call's receiver is itself a call_expression, infer its return type via the existing _infer_type_from_rust_rhs helper (Cmd::parse() -> Cmd via the associated-fn path; obj.foo() -> foo's registered return type) and resolve the outer method against Type::method in the local/global registry.\n\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE (re-ran the filed repro on the zoxide subcommand shape): main's outgoing calls are now ['Cmd::parse', 'Cmd::run'] (was ['Cmd::parse', 'Add::run'] — the chained .run() mis-resolved to a short-name sibling). Forward reachability from main via calls/dispatches_to now reaches Cmd::run: True, Query::run: True, Add::run: True — i.e. TOGETHER WITH WI-kodap this closes the original zoxide symptom end-to-end (the forward slice from main() now enters the whole subcommand tree: main -> Cmd::run -> {Query::run, Add::run} via the match arms). This resolves WI-kodap's symptom #1. rust.py at 100% coverage; smart-test 1937 passed.\n\nNote: the associated-fn heuristic (_infer_type_from_rust_rhs returns the scoped PATH for Type::assoc_fn()) assumes Self-returning constructors/parsers — correct for the dominant case; a non-Self-returning associated fn could yield an extra edge (the same ADR-0006 'extra edges over missing edges' posture the let-binding path already accepts). Dropped the resolver.lookup ambiguity fallback (unlike typed_var) because a concrete inferred type qualifies its method uniquely in local/global — kept the strategy fully covered."  # 640f
